== Chapter XVII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The strength of Sin Evidenced from its Resistance unto the power of the Law.]
]

#smallcaps[THE] measure of the strength of any #emph[Person,] or #emph[defenced City,] may be well taken from #emph[the opposition] that they are able to withstand, and not be prevailed against. If we hear of a City that hath endured #emph[a long siege] from a potent Enemy, and yet is not taken or conquered, whose walls have endured great #emph[batteries,] and are not demolished, though we have never seen the place, yet we conclude it #emph[strong,] if not impregnable.

And this Consideration will also evidence the power and strength of #emph[Indwelling-sin;] it is able #emph[to hold out,] and not only to #emph[live,] but also to secure its reign and dominion against very strong #emph[opposition] that is made unto it.

I shall instance only in #emph[the opposition that is made unto it, by the Law,] which is oft-times #emph[great and terrible,] always #emph[fruitless;] all its assaults are borne by it, and it is not prevailed against. There are sundry things wherein the Law #emph[opposeth] itself to sin, and the power of it. As,

#emph[First,] It #emph[discovers] it; sin in the Soul is like a secret #emph[hectical] distemper in the body; its being #emph[unknown] and unperceived, is one great means of its prevalency. Or as Traitors in a #emph[Civil State,] whilst they lie hid, they vigorously carry on their design. The greatest part of Men in the World, know nothing of this #emph[sickness,] yea #emph[death] of their Souls. Though they have been taught somewhat of the #emph[doctrine] of it, yet they know nothing of its #emph[power.] They know it not so, as to deal with it as their mortal Enemy. As a Man, whatever he be told, cannot be said to know, that he hath a #emph[hectical Fever,] if he love his life, and set not himself to stop its progress.

This then the Law doth, it #emph[discovers this enemy;] it convinceth the soul that there is such a traitor harboring in his bosom, Rom 7:7. #emph[I had not known sin but by the Law, for I had not known lust, except the Law had said, Thou shalt not covet.] I had not known it, that is, fully, clearly, distinctly. #emph[Conscience] will somewhat tumultuate about it; but a man cannot know it clearly and distinctly from thence. It gives a man such a sight of it, as the blind man had in the Gospel upon the first touch of his eyes, #emph[He saw men like trees walking,] obscurely, confusedly; but when the Law comes, that gives the soul #emph[a distinct sight of this indwelling-sin.] Again, #emph[I had not known it,] that is, the #emph[depths] of it, the root, the habitual inclination of my nature to sin, which is here called #emph[lust;] as it is by #emph[James,] ch. 1:14. I had not known it, or not known it to be sin, but by the Law.

This then the Law doth, it draws out this #emph[Traitor] from its secret lurking places, the intimate recesses of the soul. A man when the Law comes, is no more ignorant of his enemy; if he will now perish by him, it is openly and knowingly; he cannot but say that the Law #emph[warned] him of him, discovered him unto him, yea, and raised a #emph[concourse about him] in the soul of various affections, as an #emph[Officer] doth, that discovers a #emph[Thief] or #emph[Robber,] calling out for assistance to apprehend him.

Secondly, The Law not only #emph[discovers] sin, but discovers it to be a very #emph[bad inmate,] dangerous, yea, pernicious to the soul. Rom 7:13. #emph[Was then that which is good] (that is, the Law) #emph[made death unto me? God forbid. But sin, that it might appear sin, working death in me by that which is good, that sin by the commandment might become exceeding sinful.] There are many things in this #emph[verse] wherein we are not at present concerned; that which I only aim at, is, the #emph[manifestation] of sin by the Law; it #emph[appears to be sin;] and the manifestation of it in its own colors; it appears to be #emph[exceeding sinful.] The Law gives the soul to know the #emph[filth] and #emph[guilt] of this #emph[Indwelling-sin,] how great they are, how vile it is; what an abomination, what an enmity to God, how hated of him, the soul shall never more look upon it as a #emph[small matter,] what thoughts soever it had of it before, whereby it is greatly surprised.

As a man that finds himself somewhat distempered, sending for a #emph[Physician] of skill, when he comes, requires his judgment of his distemper. He considering his condition, tells him, Alas, I am sorry for you, the case is far otherwise with you than you imagine, your #emph[disease is mortal,] and it hath proceeded so far pressing upon your spirits, and infecting the whole #emph[mass of your blood,] that I doubt unless most effectual remedies be used, you will live but a very few hours.

So is it in this case, a man may have some #emph[trouble] in his mind and conscience about #emph[Indwelling-sin;] he finds all not well, as it should be with him, more from the #emph[effects] of sin, and its continual eruptions, than the nature of it, which he hopes to wrestle withal: but now when the #emph[Law] comes, that lets the soul know, that its disease is #emph[deadly and mortal,] that it is exceeding sinful, as being the root and cause of all his alienation from God; and thus also the Law proceeds against it.

Thirdly, The Law #emph[judgeth the person,] or lets the sinner plainly know what he is to #emph[expect] upon the account of this sin. This is the Laws proper work, its #emph[discovering] property is but #emph[preparative] to its #emph[judging.] The Law is itself, when it is in the Throne. Here it minceth not the matter with sinners, as we use to do one with another, but tells him plainly, #emph[Thou art the man,] in whom this exceeding sinful sin doth dwell, and you must answer for the guilt of it. And this, methinks, if any thing, should #emph[rouse up a man] to set himself in opposition to it, yea, utterly to destroy it. The Law lets him know that upon the account of this sin, he is obnoxious to the curse and wrath of the great God against him; yea, pronounceth the sentence of #emph[everlasting condemnation] upon him upon that account: abide in this state and perish, is its language. It leaves not the soul without this #emph[warning] in this world, and will leave it without #emph[excuse] on that account in the world to come.

Fourthly, The Law so follows on its sentence, that it #emph[disquiets] and affrights the soul, and suffers it not to enjoy the least #emph[rest] or quietness in harboring its sinful inmate. Whenever the soul hath indulged to its commands, made provision for it, immediately the Law #emph[flies upon it,] with the wrath and terror of the Lord, makes it quake and tremble: It shall have no rest, but is like a poor beast that hath a deadly arrow sticking in its sides, that makes it restless wherever it is, and whatever it doth.

Fifthly, The Law stays not here, but also it #emph[slays] the soul, Rom 7:3. That is, by its conviction of the nature, power, and desert of this #emph[indwelling-sin,] it deprives him in whom it is of all that life of #emph[self-righteousness and hope,] which formerly he sustained himself withal: It leaves him as a poor, dead, helpless, hopeless creature. And all this in the pursuit of that opposition that it makes against this sin. May we not now expect, that the power of it will be #emph[quelled,] and its strength broken; that it will #emph[die] away before these strokes of the Law of God; but the truth is, such is its power and strength, that it is quite otherwise; like him whom the #emph[Poets] feign to be born of the Earth, when one thought to slay him by casting him on the ground, by every fall he recovered new strength, and was more vigorous than formerly: So is it with all the falls and repulses that are given to #emph[Indwelling-sin] by the Law. For,

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, It is not #emph[conquered.] A conquest infers two things in respect of the conquered: First, #emph[Loss of Dominion;] and, secondly, #emph[loss of strength.] Wherever any one is #emph[conquered,] he is despoiled of both these. He loseth both his authority and his power. So the strong man armed, being prevailed against, he is #emph[bound,] and his #emph[goods] are spoiled. But now neither of these befalls #emph[Indwelling-sin] by the assaults of the Law; it loseth not one jot of its #emph[dominion] nor #emph[strength] by all the blows that are given unto it. The #emph[Law cannot] do this thing, Rom 8:3. It cannot deprive sin of its power and dominion; for he #emph[that is under the Law is also under sin;] that is, whatever power the Law gets upon the conscience of a man, so that he fears to sin, lest the sentence and curse of it should befall him, yet sin still #emph[reigns and rules] in his heart. Therefore saith the Apostle, Rom 6:14. #emph[Sin shall not have dominion over you, for you are not under the Law but under Grace;] intimating plainly that though a Person be in never so much subjection to the Authority of the Law, yet that will not exempt and acquit him from the #emph[dominion of sin.] Yea, the Law by all its work upon the Soul, instead of freeing and acquitting it from the reign of sin, and bondage unto it, doth accidentally greatly increase its misery and bondage; As the sentence of the Judge on the Bench against a Malefactor adds to his misery. The Soul is under the #emph[dominion of sin,] and it may be, abides in its woeful condition in much security; fearing neither Sin nor Judgment. The Law setting upon him in this condition by all the ways forementioned, brings him into great trouble and perplexity, fear and terror, but #emph[delivers him not at all.] So that it is with the Soul, as it was with the #emph[Israelites] when #emph[Moses] had delivered his Message unto #emph[Pharaoh,] they were so far from getting liberty by it, that their bondage was increased, #emph[and they found that they were in a very evil case;] Exod 5:19. Yea and we shall see, that sin doth like #emph[Pharaoh;] finding its rule disturbed, it grows more #emph[outrageously oppressive,] and doubles the bondage of their Souls. This is not then the work of the Law to #emph[destroy] sin, or deprive it of that dominion which it hath by nature.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Nor doth it by all these #emph[strokes of the Law] lose any thing of its #emph[strength.] It continues both its authority and its force; It is neither destroyed, nor weakened. Yea,]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, It is so far from being #emph[conquered,] that it is only #emph[enraged.] The whole work of the Law, doth only provoke and enrage sin; and cause it as it hath opportunity, to put out its strength with more power and vigor and force than formerly. This the Apostle shows at large, Rom 7:9, 10, 11, 12, 13.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But you will say, Do we not see it by experience; that many are wrought upon by the #emph[preaching of the Law] to a relinquishment of many sins, and amendment of their Lives, and to a great contending against the Eruptions of those other corruptions which they cannot yet mortify; and it cannot be denied, but that great is the power and #emph[efficacy] of the Law, when preached and applied to the Conscience in a due manner: I answer,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] It is acknowledged, that very great and effectual is the power of the Law of God. Great are the effects that are wrought by it; and it shall surely accomplish every #emph[end] for which of God it is appointed. But yet the #emph[subduing of sin,] is none of its work; it is not designed of God unto that purpose; and therefore it is no dishonor, if it cannot do that which is not its proper work, Rom 8:3.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] Whatever effects it have upon #emph[some,] yet we see that in the #emph[most,] such is the power and prevalency of sin, that it takes no impression at all upon them. May you not see everywhere men living many years in Congregations where the #emph[Law is powerfully preached,] and applied unto the Consciences as to all the ends and purposes for which the Lord is pleased to make use of it, and not once be moved by it; that receive no more impression from the strokes of it, than blows with a straw would give to an #emph[Adamant.] They are neither #emph[convinced] by it, nor #emph[terrified,] nor #emph[awed,] nor #emph[instructed,] but continue deaf, ignorant, senseless, secure, as if they had never been told of the guilt of sin, or terror of the Lord. Such as these are Congregations full of, who proclaim the #emph[triumphing power] of sin, over the dispensation of the Law.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Thirdly,] When any of the Effects mentioned are wrought, it is not from the power of #emph[the letter of the Law,] but from the actual efficacy of the Spirit of God, putting forth his virtue and power for that end and purpose; And we deny not but that the Spirit of the Lord is able to restrain and quell the power of lust when he pleaseth; and some ways whereby he is pleased so to do we have formerly considered. But,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Fourthly,] Notwithstanding all that may be observed of the #emph[power of the Law] upon the Souls of Men, yet it is most evident that Lust is not #emph[conquered,] nor subdued, nor mortified by it. For,]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] Though the #emph[course] of sin may be #emph[repelled] for a season by the dispensation of the Law, yet the #emph[Spring] and Fountain of it, is not dried up thereby, Though it withdraws and hides itself for a season, it is as I have elsewhere showed, but to #emph[shift] out of a storm, and then to return again. As a #emph[Traveler,] in his way meeting with a violent storm of thunder and rain, immediately turns out of his way, to some House or Tree for his shelter; but yet this causeth him not to #emph[give over his Journey;] so soon as the storm is over, he returns to his way and progress again. So is it with Men in #emph[bondage] unto sin: They are in a course of pursuing their lusts; the #emph[Law] meets with them in a storm of thunder and lightning from Heaven, terrifies and hinders them in their way; this turns them for a #emph[season] out of their course, they will run to prayer or amendment of life, for some shelter from the storm of wrath which is feared coming upon their Consciences. But is their course stopped, are their Principles altered? Not at all; so soon as the storm is over, that they begin to wear out that sense and the terror that was upon them, they return to their former course, in the service of sin again. This was the state with #emph[Pharaoh] once and again.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] In such seasons sin is not #emph[conquered] but #emph[diverted.] When it seems to fall under the power of the Law, indeed it is only #emph[turned] into a new channel, it is not dried up. If you go and set a #emph[dam] against the streams of a River, that you suffer no water to pass in the old course and channel, but it breaks out another way, and turns all its streams in a new course, you will not say you have #emph[dried up that River;] though some that come and look into the #emph[old channel] may think perhaps that the waters are utterly gone; so is it in this case; the streams of sin it may be run in open #emph[sensuality,] and profaneness; in drunkenness and viciousness; the #emph[preaching of the Law] sets a #emph[dam] against these courses; Conscience is terrified, and the Man dares not walk in the ways wherein he hath been formerly engaged; His #emph[Companions] in sin not finding him in his #emph[old ways] begin to laugh at him, as one that is #emph[converted] and growing precise; Professors themselves begin to be persuaded, that the work of God is upon his heart, because they see his old streams dried up; but if there have been only a #emph[work of the Law] upon him, there is a #emph[dam] put to his course, but the #emph[Spring of sin] is not dried up, only the streams of it are turned another way: It may be the Man is fallen upon other more #emph[secret,] or more #emph[spiritual] sins; or if he be beat from them also, the whole strength of lust and sin will take up its residence in self-righteousness, and pour out thereby as filthy streams as in any other way whatever. So that notwithstanding the #emph[whole work of the Law] upon the Souls of Men, #emph[Indwelling-sin] will keep #emph[alive] in them still, which is another evidence of its great power and strength.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I shall yet touch upon some other evidences of the same truth, that I have under consideration; but I shall be brief in them: In the next place then, the great #emph[endeavors of Men ignorant of the Righteousness of Christ] for the #emph[subduing] and mortifying of sin, which are all fruitless, do evidence the great strength and power of it.]

Men who have no #emph[strength against sin,] may yet be made sensible of the #emph[strength of sin.] The way whereby for the most part they come to that knowledge, is by some previous sense that they have of the #emph[guilt] of sin. This Men have by the Light of their Consciences; they cannot avoid it: This is not a thing in their choice, whether they will or no, they cannot but know sin to be evil, and that such an evil that renders them obnoxious to the Judgment of God. This galls the Minds and Consciences of some so far, as that they are kept in awe, and dare not sin as they would. Being awed with a sense of the guilt of sin, and the terror of the Lord, Men begin to endeavor to #emph[abstain from sin,] at least from such sins as they have been most terrified about. Whilst they have this design in hand, the strength and power of sin begins to discover itself unto them; They begin to find that there is #emph[something in them,] that is not in their own power; for notwithstanding their resolutions and purposes, they #emph[sin still;] and that so, or in such a manner, as that their Consciences inform them that they must therefore perish eternally. This puts them on #emph[self-endeavours] to suppress the eruption of sin, because they cannot be quiet unless so they do; nor have any rest or peace within. Now being ignorant of that only way whereby sin is to be mortified, that is #emph[by the Spirit of Christ,] they fix on many ways in their own strength to suppress it, if not to slay it; as being ignorant of that only way whereby Consciences burdened with the guilt of sin may be pacified, that is by the blood of Christ; they endeavor by many other ways to accomplish that end in vain; for no Man by any self-endeavours can obtain peace with God. Some of the ways whereby they endeavor to suppress the power of sin, which casts them into an unquiet condition, and their insufficiency for that end, we must look into.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] They will promise and bind themselves by #emph[Vows] from those sins, which they have been most liable unto, and so have been most perplexed withal. The #emph[Psalmist] shows this to be one great Engine whereby false and #emph[hypocritical] Persons do endeavor to extricate and deliver themselves out of trouble and perplexity; They make #emph[Promises to God;] which he calls, #emph[flattering him with the lips,] Ps 78:36. So is it in this case; being freshly galled with the guilt of any sin, that by the power of their temptations, they, it may be, have frequently been overtaken in, they #emph[vow] and #emph[promise,] that at least for some #emph[such space of time] as they will limit, they will not commit that sin again, and this course of proceeding is prescribed unto them by some who pretend to direct their Consciences in this Duty. Conscience of this, now makes them watch over themselves as to the outward act of the sin that they are galled with; and so it hath one of these two effects; for either they do abstain from it for the time they have prefixed; or they do not: If they do not, as seldom they do, especially if it be a sin that hath a peculiar root in their nature and constitution, and is improved by custom into an habit, if any suitable temptation be presented unto them; their sin is increased, and therewith their terror, and they are woefully discouraged in making any opposition to sin; and therefore for the most part after #emph[one] or two vain attempts, or more it may be, knowing no other way to mortify sin, but this of #emph[vowing] against it, and keeping of that Vow in their own strength, they give over #emph[all contests,] and become wholly the servants of sin, being bounded only by outward considerations, without any serious endeavors for a recovery. Or #emph[Secondly,] suppose that they have success in their Resolutions, and do abstain from actual sins their appointed season; commonly one of these two things ensue; either they think that they have #emph[well discharged their Duty,] and so may a little now, at least for a season, indulge to their corruptions, and lusts, and so are entangled again in the same snares of sin as formerly; or else they reckon that their #emph[vow and promise] hath preserved them, and so #emph[sacrifice to their own net and drag,] setting up a Righteousness of their own against the grace of God; which is so far from weakening #emph[Indwelling-sin,] that it strengthens it in the root and Principle, that it may hereafter reign in the Soul in security. Or at the most, the best success that can be imagined unto this way of dealing with sin, is but the #emph[restraining] of some outward eruptions of it, which tends nothing to the weakening of its power; and therefore such persons by all their endeavors are very far from being freed from the inward #emph[toiling, burning,] disquieting, perplexing power of sin. And this is the state of most Men that are kept in #emph[bondage] under the power of #emph[conviction;] Hell, Death, and the Wrath of God are continually presented unto their #emph[Consciences;] this makes them labor with all their strength against that in sin which most enrageth their Consciences, and most increaseth their fears; that is the #emph[actual eruption] of it; for, for the most part while they are freed from that, they are safe; though in the meantime, sin lie tumultuating in and defiling of the heart continually. As with running sores, outward #emph[repelling medicines] may skin them over, and hinder their corruption from coming forth; but the issue of them is, that they cause them to #emph[fester] inwardly, and so prove, though it may be not so #emph[noisome] and offensive as they were before, yet far more #emph[dangerous.] So is it with this repelling of the power of corruption by #emph[Men's Vows,] and promises against it; #emph[external eruptions] are it may be restrained for a season; but the #emph[inward root] and principle is not weakened in the least. And most commonly this is the issue of this way; that #emph[sin] having gotten more strength, and being enraged by its restraint, breaks all its bounds, and captivates the Soul unto all filthy abominations; which is the Principle as was before observed of most of the #emph[visible Apostasies] which we have in the World, 2 Pet 2:19, 20.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The Holy Ghost compares sinners, because of the #emph[odious,] fierce, poisonous nature of this #emph[Indwelling-sin,] unto #emph[Lions, Bears,] and #emph[Asps,] Isa 11:6, 7, 8, 9. Now this is the excellency of Gospel-grace, that it changes the #emph[Nature] and inward Principles of these otherwise passionate and untamed Beasts, making the #emph[Wolf] as the #emph[Kid,] the #emph[Lion] as the #emph[Lamb,] and the #emph[Bear] as the #emph[Cow.] When this is effected, they may safely be trusted in; a #emph[little Child may lead them:] But these #emph[self-endeavours] do not at all change the Nature, but restrain their outward violence: He that takes a #emph[Lion,] or a #emph[Wolf,] and shuts him up from ravening, whilst yet his inward violence remains, may well expect that at one time or other they will break their bonds, and fall to their former ways of rapine and violence. However, shutting them up, doth not as we see, change their Natures, but only restrain their #emph[rage] from doing open spoil. So it is in this case; it is Grace alone that #emph[changeth the heart,] and takes away that poison and fierceness that is in them by nature; Men's #emph[self-endeavours] do but coerce them as to some outward eruptions. But,]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] Beyond bare #emph[Vows and Promises] with some watchfulness to observe them in a rational use of ordinary means, Men have put, and some do yet put themselves on #emph[extraordinary ways] of mortifying sin. This is the foundation of all that hath #emph[a show of Wisdom and Religion] in the Papacy; their hours of Prayer, Fastings, their immuring and cloistering themselves, their Pilgrimages, Penances, and self-torturing Discipline, spring all from this root. I shall not speak of the #emph[innumerable evils] that have attended these self-invented ways of #emph[Mortification,] and how they all of them have been turned into means, occasions, and advantages of sinning; nor of #emph[the horrible hypocrisy] which evidently cleaves unto the most of their Observers; nor of that #emph[superstition] which gives life to them all, being a thing riveted in the natures of some, and their constitutions, fixed on others by inveterate prejudices; and the same by others taken up for secular advantages; but I will suppose the best that can be made of it, and it will be found to be #emph[a self-invented design] of Men ignorant of the Righteousness of God, to give a check to this power of #emph[Indwelling-sin] whereof we speak. And it's almost incredible, what fearful #emph[self-macerations,] and horrible sufferings this design hath carried Men out unto: And undoubtedly their #emph[blind zeal and superstition] will rise in judgment, and condemn the horrible sloth and negligence of the most of them to whom the Lord hath granted the saving Light of the Gospel. But what is the end of these things? The Apostle in brief gives us an account; Rom 9:31, 32. #emph[They attain not the Righteousness aimed at;] they come not up unto a conformity to the Law; #emph[sin is not mortified;] no nor the power of it weakened; but what it loseth in sensual, in carnal pleasures, it takes up with great advantage, in blindness, darkness, superstition, self-righteousness, and soul-pride, contempt of the Gospel, and the righteousness of it, and reigns no less, than in the most profligate sinners in the World.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Lastly, The strength, efficacy and power of this Law of Sin, may be farther evidenced from its life and in-being in the Soul, notwithstanding the wound that is given unto it, in the first conversion of the Soul to God; and the continual opposition that is made unto it by Grace. But this is the subject and design of another Endeavor.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It may now be expected, that we should here add the especial #emph[Uses] of all this discovery that hath been made of the #emph[Power, Deceit, Prevalency] and #emph[Success,] of this great Adversary of our Souls. But as for what concerns that Humility, self-Abasement, Watchfulness, Diligence, and Application unto the Lord Christ for relief, which will become those who find in themselves by Experience the power of this Law of Sin, have been occasionally mentioned and inculcated through the whole preceding Discourse, so for what concerns the actual #emph[Mortification] of it, I shall only recommend unto the Reader for his Direction, another small Treatise written long since unto that purpose, which I suppose he may do well to consider together with this, if he find these things to be his Concernment.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[To the Only Wise God our Savior, be Glory and Majesty, Dominion, and Power, both Now and Ever.] Amen.]]
